  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
         comment__text babybear:mt-0.5" dir="ltr">Independent verification only bites if the verifier controls what the agent is allowed to touch while it iterates, not just whether the output clears the finish line. A self-correcting loop that runs unattended on a schedule will happily pursue a provable stop condition using data access and system actions no one scoped for that run. In practice the missing primitive is authorization at runtime, which permissions the loop holds, which actions require a human, and what evidence each iteration leaves behind for later review. Your point that you can delegate iteration but never taste is right, and the operational version of taste is a policy the runtime enforces, not a review step after the agent already acted. Goals and loops make agents capable, but the durable layer is control over what those loops are permitted to do.</p>
